# High-Level Design: Server-Authoritative Duel Typing Game

**Stack:** Next.js (App Router) · Prisma (Postgres/Supabase) · Upstash Redis  
**Real-time Strategy:** REST Polling every 2 s · CSS transition ghost smoothing  
**Auth Model:** Server is the single source of truth for clock, progress, and game state.

---

## A. Redis Schema & State Machine

### Hash Structure: `race:{roomId}`

```
race:{roomId}
├── state            STRING  — current FSM state (see below)
├── host_id          STRING  — userId of the host
├── guest_id         STRING  — userId of the guest (empty until join)
├── prompt_id        STRING  — ID of the selected text prompt
├── prompt_text      STRING  — cached prompt text (avoids DB round-trip)
├── host_ready       BOOLEAN — "0" | "1"
├── guest_ready      BOOLEAN — "0" | "1"
├── target_start_ms  INTEGER — Unix ms of when the race will begin (server clock)
├── created_at_ms    INTEGER — creation timestamp (for TTL housekeeping)
│
├── host_progress    INTEGER — chars typed correctly by host
├── guest_progress   INTEGER — chars typed correctly by guest
├── host_wpm         INTEGER — latest reported WPM for host
├── guest_wpm        INTEGER — latest reported WPM for guest
├── host_last_active INTEGER — Unix ms of last /sync call by host
├── guest_last_active INTEGER — Unix ms of last /sync call by guest
│
├── host_finished_ms  INTEGER — Unix ms host crossed finish line (0 if not done)
├── guest_finished_ms INTEGER — Unix ms guest crossed finish line (0 if not done)
├── winner_id         STRING  — conclusive winner userId (written once)
└── persisted_to_db   STRING  — "1" once the RaceResult Prisma write succeeds
```

**TTL Policy:** `EXPIRE race:{roomId} 3600` — rooms auto-evict after 1 hour.

> [!NOTE]
> All values returned by `HGETALL` are **strings**. Callers must explicitly cast integers (`parseInt(...)`) and booleans (`=== "1"`). See the `parseRaceData` helper in Section D.

---

### State Machine

```
                           Guest joins
WAITING_FOR_GUEST ──────────────────────► LOBBY_FULL
                                              │
                              Both players    │  Player 2 never Readies
                              click "Ready"   │  → AUTO_ABANDON (TTL-based)
                                              ▼
                                          READY_CHECK
                                              │
                              Both ready=1    │
                                              ▼
                                          COUNTDOWN
                                    (target_start_ms set)
                                              │
                              target_start_ms reached
                                              ▼
                                          IN_PROGRESS
                                              │
                              First/both players finish
                                              ▼
                                           FINISHED
```

| Transition | Trigger | Actor |
|---|---|---|
| `WAITING_FOR_GUEST → LOBBY_FULL` | Guest POSTs `/race/join` | Server |
| `LOBBY_FULL → READY_CHECK` | Both set `ready=1` via `/race/ready` | Server |
| `READY_CHECK → COUNTDOWN` | Host POSTs `/race/start`; server sets `target_start_ms` | Server |
| `COUNTDOWN → IN_PROGRESS` | First `/race/sync` call where `now >= target_start_ms` | Server |
| `IN_PROGRESS → FINISHED` | A player submits 100% progress | Server |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> State transitions are guarded by Redis `WATCH` / Lua scripts to prevent race conditions. No client can skip a state.

---

## B. Heartbeat & Ghosting Logic

### Mechanism: `last_active` Timestamp

Every `/api/race/sync` (the "Pulse") writes `host_last_active` or `guest_last_active` to the current server time. Since polling is every **2 s**, a **5-second threshold** (2–3 missed polls) is the disconnect signal.

```
DISCONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000
```

### Server-Side Detection (checked on every /sync call)

```typescript
const now = Date.now(); // resolved from redis.time() in production
const opponentLastActive = parseInt(raceData.guest_last_active ?? "0");

if (now - opponentLastActive > DISCONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS && state === "IN_PROGRESS") {
  // Mark opponent as disconnected
  await redis.hset(`race:${roomId}`, {
    state: "FINISHED",
    winner_id: currentPlayerId, // caller wins by forfeit
  });
}
```

### Client-Side Ghost Rendering

The opponent's `progress` value returned in each `/sync` response drives a CSS transform:

```css
.ghost-car {
  left: calc(var(--opponent-progress) * 1%);
  transition: left 2s linear; /* smooths the 2s polling jumps */
}
```

The `transition: left 2s linear` exactly matches the polling interval, so the ghost appears to move continuously between data points.

### What Clients Display on Disconnect

If the calling player's own `last_active` hasn't been updated (i.e. the tab went to background and polling stopped), the **server** will also eventually time them out. From the *opponent's* perspective, the ghost car stops moving and a "Opponent disconnected" overlay appears when their `/sync` response includes `winner_id`.

---

## C. The Sync Protocol — Server-Clock Offset

### Problem
Client system clocks can drift or be manually altered. The server must own the countdown.

### Solution: `targetStartTime` + Client Offset

#### Step 1 — Host triggers start: `POST /api/race/start`

```typescript
// Server-side
const [serverSec, serverMicros] = await redis.time(); // Upstash redis.time()
const serverNowMs = serverSec * 1000 + Math.floor(serverMicros / 1000);
const COUNTDOWN_DURATION_MS = 10_000;
const targetStartMs = serverNowMs + COUNTDOWN_DURATION_MS;

await redis.hset(`race:${roomId}`, {
  state: "COUNTDOWN",
  target_start_ms: targetStartMs,
});
```

#### Step 2 — Server response includes server's current time

Every `/sync` response includes two fields:

```json
{
  "serverNowMs": 1742553158000,
  "targetStartMs": 1742553168000,
  "state": "COUNTDOWN",
  ...
}
```

#### Step 3 — Client calculates its local offset

```typescript
// Recomputed on EVERY /sync response (not just the first)
const clockOffsetMs = serverNowMs - Date.now(); // can be negative

// Each local tick:
const adjustedNow = Date.now() + clockOffsetMs;
const msRemaining = targetStartMs - adjustedNow;
```

This means **even if the user sets their clock back 5 minutes**, `clockOffsetMs` corrects for it on every poll. The countdown displayed is always relative to the server's real elapsed time.

#### Step 4 — Transition to IN_PROGRESS

```typescript
// Server checks on every /sync:
if (state === "COUNTDOWN" && serverNowMs >= targetStartMs) {
  await redis.hset(`race:${roomId}`, { state: "IN_PROGRESS" });
}
```

Clients independently flip to active input mode based on **TrueTime**, not on the Redis `state` field:

```typescript
// CLIENT — do NOT gate on raceData.state === "IN_PROGRESS"
// Gate directly on adjusted time, the Redis state is a lagging indicator
const adjustedNow = Date.now() + clockOffsetMs;
const raceIsLive = adjustedNow >= targetStartMs;

if (raceIsLive) {
  enableInput(); // always safe — server rejects pre-start keystrokes anyway
}
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **First-Sync Loophole fix:** If neither player polls for several seconds after `targetStartMs` elapses, Redis state stays `COUNTDOWN` but time is already running. The client must enable input as soon as `adjustedNow >= targetStartMs`, regardless of what the Redis `state` field says. The server will confirm `IN_PROGRESS` on the very next `/sync`.

---

## D. API Contract Specifications

### `POST /api/race/create`

**Purpose:** Host creates a new room and initializes Redis state.

**Auth:** Required (session token).

**Request Body:**
```json
{
  "promptId": "prompt_abc123"
}
```

**Server Logic:**
1. Generate `roomId = nanoid(10)`.
2. Fetch `promptText` from Prisma by `promptId`.
3. Get server time via `redis.time()`.
4. Atomically `HSET race:{roomId}` with all fields, `state = "WAITING_FOR_GUEST"`, `host_id = userId`.
5. Set `EXPIRE race:{roomId} 3600`.
6. Return `{ roomId, shareCode }`.

**Response:**
```json
{
  "roomId": "xK9mL2pQrT",
  "shareCode": "xK9mL2pQrT",
  "state": "WAITING_FOR_GUEST"
}
```

---

### `PATCH /api/race/join`

**Purpose:** Guest joins an existing room.

**Auth:** Required.

**Request Body:**
```json
{
  "roomId": "xK9mL2pQrT"
}
```

**Concurrency Guard (preventing 3rd players):**

Use a Redis Lua script to make the check-and-set atomic:

```lua
-- Lua script executed atomically on Redis
local key = KEYS[1]
local guestId = ARGV[1]
local state = redis.call('HGET', key, 'state')
local existingGuest = redis.call('HGET', key, 'guest_id')

if state ~= 'WAITING_FOR_GUEST' then
  return redis.error_reply('ROOM_NOT_AVAILABLE')
end
if existingGuest and existingGuest ~= '' then
  return redis.error_reply('ROOM_FULL')
end

redis.call('HSET', key, 'guest_id', guestId, 'state', 'LOBBY_FULL')
return 'OK'
```

This eliminates the TOCTOU window where two guests could join simultaneously.

**Response (success):**
```json
{
  "roomId": "xK9mL2pQrT",
  "state": "LOBBY_FULL",
  "promptText": "The quick brown fox..."
}
```

**Response (failure):**
```json
{ "error": "ROOM_FULL" }  // HTTP 409
```

---

### `POST /api/race/sync` — The Pulse

**Purpose:** Single round-trip that writes player progress AND reads opponent progress and full game state.

**Auth:** Required. `userId` is extracted from the session.

**Request Body:**
```json
{
  "roomId": "xK9mL2pQrT",
  "progress": 47,
  "wpm": 82
}
```

**`parseRaceData` Helper — Fix for HGETALL String Types:**

All `HGETALL` values arrive as strings. Centralise casting here to avoid `0 + 1 = "01"` bugs:

```typescript
function parseRaceData(raw: Record<string, string>) {
  return {
    ...raw,
    // booleans stored as "0" | "1"
    host_ready:         raw.host_ready === "1",
    guest_ready:        raw.guest_ready === "1",
    persisted_to_db:    raw.persisted_to_db === "1",
    // integers
    host_progress:      parseInt(raw.host_progress  ?? "0"),
    guest_progress:     parseInt(raw.guest_progress ?? "0"),
    host_wpm:           parseInt(raw.host_wpm       ?? "0"),
    guest_wpm:          parseInt(raw.guest_wpm      ?? "0"),
    host_last_active:   parseInt(raw.host_last_active  ?? "0"),
    guest_last_active:  parseInt(raw.guest_last_active ?? "0"),
    host_finished_ms:   parseInt(raw.host_finished_ms  ?? "0"),
    guest_finished_ms:  parseInt(raw.guest_finished_ms ?? "0"),
    target_start_ms:    parseInt(raw.target_start_ms   ?? "0"),
    ready_deadline_ms:  parseInt(raw.ready_deadline_ms ?? "0"),
  };
}
```

**Server Logic (single atomic pipeline):**

```typescript
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const { roomId, progress, wpm } = await req.json();
  const userId = getSessionUserId(req);

  const key = `race:${roomId}`;

  // 1. Determine if caller is host or guest
  const [hostId, guestId] = await redis.hmget(key, "host_id", "guest_id");
  const isHost = userId === hostId;
  const role = isHost ? "host" : "guest";
  const opponentRole = isHost ? "guest" : "host";

  // 2. Get server time (authoritative)
  const [sec, micros] = await redis.time();
  const serverNowMs = sec * 1000 + Math.floor(micros / 1000);

  // 3. Write own progress + last_active; read entire hash in one pipeline
  const pipeline = redis.pipeline();
  pipeline.hset(key, {
    [`${role}_progress`]: progress,
    [`${role}_wpm`]: wpm,
    [`${role}_last_active`]: serverNowMs,
  });
  pipeline.hgetall(key);
  const [, rawData] = await pipeline.exec();
  const raceData = parseRaceData(rawData as Record<string, string>);

  // 4. Heartbeat disconnect detection
  if (
    raceData.state === "IN_PROGRESS" &&
    serverNowMs - raceData[`${opponentRole}_last_active` as keyof typeof raceData] as number > DISCONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
  ) {
    await redis.hset(key, { state: "FINISHED", winner_id: userId });
    raceData.state = "FINISHED";
    raceData.winner_id = userId;
  }

  // 5. Check if COUNTDOWN has elapsed (server-side guard)
  // Note: clients may already be running based on TrueTime; server confirms here
  if (raceData.state === "COUNTDOWN" && serverNowMs >= raceData.target_start_ms) {
    await redis.hset(key, { state: "IN_PROGRESS" });
    raceData.state = "IN_PROGRESS";
  }

  // 6. Check finish condition
  if (raceData.state === "IN_PROGRESS" && progress >= 100 && !raceData[`${role}_finished_ms`]) {
    await redis.hset(key, { [`${role}_finished_ms`]: serverNowMs });
    (raceData as Record<string, unknown>)[`${role}_finished_ms`] = serverNowMs;
    // Winner resolution runs here — see Section E
  }

  // 7. Build response
  return Response.json({
    state: raceData.state,
    serverNowMs,
    targetStartMs: raceData.target_start_ms,
    myProgress: progress,
    opponentProgress: raceData[`${opponentRole}_progress` as keyof typeof raceData],
    opponentWpm: raceData[`${opponentRole}_wpm` as keyof typeof raceData],
    winnerId: raceData.winner_id ?? null,
  });
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> The single `pipeline.exec()` call keeps write + read to **one network round-trip** to Upstash, minimizing latency on the free tier.

---

## E. Conflict Resolution — True Winner Determination

### Scenario: Both players finish within the same 2-second polling window

Because polling is asynchronous, two `/sync` calls with `progress = 100` can arrive within milliseconds of each other. The winner is determined by **server-recorded finish time**, not by who called the API first.

```typescript
// Called when state === "IN_PROGRESS" && progress >= 100
async function resolveWinner(key: string, role: string, serverNowMs: number, raceData: Record<string, string>) {
  const myFinishedKey = `${role}_finished_ms`;
  const opponentRole = role === "host" ? "guest" : "host";
  const opponentFinishedKey = `${opponentRole}_finished_ms`;

  // Atomically set my finish time only if not already set
  const luaSetFinish = `
    local existing = redis.call('HGET', KEYS[1], ARGV[1])
    if existing == false or existing == '' then
      redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], ARGV[1], ARGV[2])
      return 1
    end
    return 0
  `;
  await redis.eval(luaSetFinish, [key], [myFinishedKey, serverNowMs.toString()]);

  // Re-fetch both finish times
  const [myMs, opponentMs] = await redis.hmget(key, myFinishedKey, opponentFinishedKey);

  const myTime = parseInt(myMs ?? "0");
  const oppTime = parseInt(opponentMs ?? "0");

  let winnerId: string;

  if (myTime > 0 && oppTime > 0) {
    // Both finished — compare server timestamps
    const myId = raceData[`${role}_id`] ?? raceData.host_id;
    const oppId = raceData[`${opponentRole}_id`] ?? raceData.guest_id;
    winnerId = myTime <= oppTime ? myId : oppId;
    await redis.hset(key, { state: "FINISHED", winner_id: winnerId });

    // ── Dual-Write Guard ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Use a Lua CAS to flip persisted_to_db "0" → "1" atomically.
    // Only the first caller executes the Prisma write; the second gets 0 back.
    const luaPersist = `
      local v = redis.call('HGET', KEYS[1], 'persisted_to_db')
      if v == '1' then return 0 end
      redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'persisted_to_db', '1')
      return 1
    `;
    const shouldPersist = await redis.eval(luaPersist, [key], []);
    if (shouldPersist === 1) {
      // Safe to write — we won the race
      try {
        await persistRaceResult(key, raceData, winnerId);
      } catch (err) {
        // Roll back the flag so the next /sync call can retry
        await redis.hset(key, { persisted_to_db: "0" });
        console.error("Prisma write failed, will retry on next poll", err);
      }
    }
    // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  }
  // If only one has finished, state remains IN_PROGRESS for opponent to finish
}
```

**Tie-break rule:** The player whose `finished_ms` is numerically smaller wins. Since this is the server's `redis.time()`, it is immune to client clock manipulation.

---

## F. Edge Case Handling

### Edge Case 1 — Guest joins but never clicks "Ready"

**Problem:** `state = LOBBY_FULL`, `guest_ready = 0`. Game is stalled.

**Solution: Ready-Check Timeout**

- When the room transitions to `LOBBY_FULL`, server sets `ready_deadline_ms = serverNowMs + 60_000` (1 minute).
- Each `/sync` or `/ready` call checks: if `serverNowMs > ready_deadline_ms && guest_ready == 0`:
  - Transition state → `ABANDONED`.
  - Client polling receives `state: "ABANDONED"`, shows "Opponent failed to ready up" UI.
  - Host is redirected to lobby with no penalty.

```typescript
if (state === "LOBBY_FULL" && serverNowMs > parseInt(raceData.ready_deadline_ms ?? "0")) {
  await redis.hset(key, { state: "ABANDONED" });
}
```

---

### Edge Case 2 — Host's "Start" request arrives at Redis after Guest's poll has already read `COUNTDOWN`

**Problem:** Race condition ordering — guest polls and sees `state = READY_CHECK`, then host's `/race/start` writes `state = COUNTDOWN` with `target_start_ms`. The guest's next poll (2 s later) will catch up.

**Why this is safe:** The `target_start_ms` is set to `serverNowMs + 10,000`. Even if the guest's poll arrives 2 seconds after the start was triggered, they receive `targetStartMs` with ~8 seconds remaining. The client computes `clockOffsetMs` fresh on this response and syncs correctly.

**Guarantee:** No client will ever miss the countdown because:
1. The countdown window is 10 s — much longer than the 2 s polling interval.
2. The client re-derives `msRemaining` from server time on every poll.

---

### Edge Case 3 — Clock Drift (user manually changes local machine time during race)

**Problem:** User sets their clock back 10 minutes mid-race to extend their perceived countdown.

**Why the design is immune:**

```typescript
// Recalculated on EVERY /sync response
const clockOffsetMs = serverNowMs - Date.now();
const adjustedNow = Date.now() + clockOffsetMs;
const msRemaining = targetStartMs - adjustedNow;
```

If the user sets their clock back, `Date.now()` decreases, so `clockOffsetMs` becomes a larger positive number, and `adjustedNow` remains approximately the same. The displayed countdown stays correct.

**Server-side enforcement:** Even if a client sends keystrokes during a manipulated countdown, the server rejects progress updates when `state !== "IN_PROGRESS"`. The authoritative transition to `IN_PROGRESS` only happens when `redis.time() >= targetStartMs`.

---

## G. Complete Flow Diagram

```
HOST                        SERVER (Redis)                    GUEST
 │                               │                               │
 │── POST /race/create ──────────►│  state=WAITING_FOR_GUEST     │
 │◄─ { roomId } ─────────────────│                               │
 │                               │◄─── PATCH /race/join ─────────│
 │                               │  state=LOBBY_FULL             │
 │                               │──── { promptText } ──────────►│
 │── POST /race/ready ───────────►│  host_ready=1                │
 │                               │◄─── POST /race/ready ─────────│
 │                               │  guest_ready=1                │
 │                               │  state=READY_CHECK             │
 │── POST /race/start ───────────►│  state=COUNTDOWN              │
 │                               │  target_start_ms=now+10000    │
 │◄── poll /race/sync ───────────│  returns serverNowMs          │
 │  (clockOffset computed)       │◄─── poll /race/sync ──────────│
 │                               │                               │
 │  [10s countdown elapses]      │                               │
 │                               │  state=IN_PROGRESS            │
 │── POST /race/sync (prog=47) ──►│  writes host_progress=47     │
 │◄── { oppProgress=52 } ────────│  reads guest_progress=52      │
 │                               │◄─── POST /race/sync (p=52) ───│
 │                               │◄─── POST /race/sync (p=100) ──│
 │                               │  guest_finished_ms=T1         │
 │── POST /race/sync (prog=100) ─►│  host_finished_ms=T2         │
 │                               │  T1<T2 → winner=guest_id      │
 │◄── { winnerId: guest_id } ────│──── { winnerId: guest_id } ──►│
 │                               │  state=FINISHED               │
 │                               │  → Prisma write (RaceResult)  │
```

---

## H. Adaptive Polling Strategy (Free-Tier Budget)

Upstash free tier = **10,000 requests/day**. With 2 players polling every 2 s for 60 s, each race costs **60 requests**. Cap: ~166 races/day before throttling.

**Solution: State-Gated Poll Interval**

```typescript
// Client-side polling hook
function getPollInterval(state: RaceState): number {
  switch (state) {
    case "WAITING_FOR_GUEST":
    case "LOBBY_FULL":
      return 5_000; // 5 s — lobby states, nothing time-critical
    case "READY_CHECK":
      return 3_000; // 3 s — slightly tighter, waiting for both readies
    case "COUNTDOWN":
    case "IN_PROGRESS":
      return 2_000; // 2 s — race is live, minimum latency
    default:
      return 10_000; // FINISHED / ABANDONED — slow polling until redirect
  }
}
```

**Revised Budget per Race:**

| Phase | Duration | Interval | Requests (2 players) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Lobby / Ready | ~30 s | 5 s | 12 |
| Countdown | 10 s | 2 s | 10 |
| In Progress | ~60 s | 2 s | 60 |
| **Total** | | | **~82 requests** |

This extends the free-tier cap to **~243 races/day** — a ~46% improvement with no UX regression.

---

## I. Summary of Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| Redis Hash per room | Single key lookup; atomic HSET for all fields |
| Lua scripts for join & finish | Eliminates TOCTOU race conditions without distributed locks |
| `redis.time()` for server clock | Unaffected by host OS clock drift; atomic with Redis ops |
| `parseRaceData()` helper | Centralised casting prevents `parseInt` bugs on HGETALL strings |
| `persisted_to_db` Lua CAS flag | Ensures exactly-once Prisma write; rollback on failure |
| Client TrueTime gate (not Redis state) | Prevents lost racing seconds if first poll is delayed |
| Adaptive poll interval by state | ~46% fewer Upstash requests vs. fixed 2 s polling |
| 2 s poll + CSS `transition 2s linear` | Simulates smooth ghost movement at zero WebSocket cost |
| `clockOffsetMs` recalculated each poll | Corrects for in-flight clock drift continuously |
| `finished_ms` timestamps for winner | Fair, tamper-proof tiebreaker within same poll window |
| TTL = 3600 s | Free-tier memory bounded; stale rooms self-evict |
| Disconnect threshold = 5 s (2–3 missed polls) | Balances false positives vs. real disconnects |
